    ('homelessness-response', 3, 'Pratt''s citywide strategy pairs reasonable public-space rules with services and behavioral-health capacity: she voted for the unanimous June 26, 2023 camping ordinance that limits camping to a designated lot near the police station between 7 p.m. and 7 a.m. while leaving camping lawful when a person is without available shelter, and the city administers the regulations within its broader homelessness program. In her September 2024 reelection interview she named mental health services — alongside transportation and housing — as one of the three key challenges facing Tualatin, and her housing record (the Tualatin Heights rezoning vote and urban renewal districts she credits with adding over 400 housing units) treats supply as part of the response. Nothing in her record supports enforcement-first crackdowns or minimizing city spending on services, nor a no-enforcement housing-first-only posture; the documented mix is outreach-and-services investment alongside enforceable public-space rules.', ARRAY['https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/new-ordinance-limits-hours-and-locations-for-houseless-camping/', 'https://beavertonvalleytimes.com/2024/09/13/pratt-seeks-second-term-on-tualatin-city-council/', 'https://tualatinoregon.gov/administration/homelessness/camping-regulations/']::text[]),
    ('climate-change', 3, 'Pratt cast one of the three dissenting votes (with Councilors Brooks and Sacco) when the council voted 4-3 in September 2022 to join thirteen local governments suing to pause Oregon''s Climate Friendly and Equitable Communities rules, stating "My decision is based on what I think is best for the climate" and arguing the city''s resources were better spent meeting climate goals than litigating against them. In her September 2024 reelection interview she listed "implementing Tualatin''s Climate Action Plan" — the council-adopted May 2024 roadmap toward citywide net-zero emissions by 2050 through energy efficiency, renewable energy, and more sustainable transportation — as a top priority for her next term. Her documented posture is affirmative local climate action on a gradual net-zero-2050 trajectory: she has not advocated a 2030 fossil-fuel phase-out or emergency declaration, but she has actively refused to obstruct state climate mandates. That matches the invest-in-clean-energy-while-gradually-reducing-fossil-fuel-reliance chair.', ARRAY['https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/tualatin-among-13-cities-suing-oregon-to-halt-new-climate-regulations/', 'https://beavertonvalleytimes.com/2024/09/13/pratt-seeks-second-term-on-tualatin-city-council/']::text[]),
